  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Monday)\nIssued at 1206 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nHigh pressure currently prevails across the Mid-South as a trough\ndeepens over northern Texas. Despite high pressure prevailing,\nbrisk northerly winds will keep temperatures on the cooler side\ntoday with highs ranging from the upper 40s to upper 50s. Some\nareas across north Mississippi may hit 60 degrees briefly, but\ncloud cover from the approaching system is likely to aid in the\ncool solution.\n\nThe deep trough will gradually continue to push towards the\nregion and initiate shower development overnight into Wednesday\nmorning. QPF amounts have continued to increase as the system\nwill undergo a phasing period with an upper level low currently\nover the northern Plains. During this phasing period, expect the\ntrack and speed of the system to shift and slow. This will allow\nfor steady stratiform rainfall over much of the area albeit, the\nsystem does not appear very well forced at the onset and upper\nair analysis shows plentiful dry air throughout the atmosphere\nQPF values have been trimmed downwards in this overnight period\nto account for this dry air.\n\nThe cloud cover and subtle WAA ahead of this system should keep\ntemperatures at the surface above freezing with the onset\ntomorrow morning, however, after sunset on Wednesday, a few\nflakes could mix in across the MO/AR and TN/KY borders. There is\nlow confidence in impacts from this as model soundings do not\nindicate efficient saturation in the DGZ and due to above\nfreezing surface temperatures. The phasing of the upper low and\ntrough will continue to keep showers in the forecast for the\naforementioned areas into early Thanksgiving morning. Depending\non the track once phasing is complete, some lingering\nprecipitation could even hang around until late evening, but\namounts will be light as the system lifts away from the area.\nBottom line up front, prepare for some beneficial rainfall areas\neast of the Mississippi River, particularly across the Tennessee\nRiver Valley, where an inch or more of rain is not out of the\nquestion. A few snowflakes, with low potential for a dusting,\ncould occur in extreme northeast Arkansas, the Missouri Bootheel,\nand northwest Tennessee on Thanksgiving morning. It is important\nto note that as hi-resolution guidance comes into the viewing\nzone, there are a few model solutions that want to bring an inch,\npossibly up to 2\" of snowfall across the aforementioned area.\nThese solutions do provide a higher moisture content in the DGZ\nand slightly colder surface temperatures, which is good reasoning\nfor the higher accumulation amounts. While this scenario is less\nlikely, it is not completely out of the question. This is a low\nprobability, high impact situation and will be very dependent on\nthe phasing of the two systems.  There may be a Winter Weather\nAdvisory issued once we can see the track of the system, but\nconfidence is too low to issue anything at this time.\n\nOnce this system clears the area on Friday, we will have a brief\ndry spell ahead of another late weekend system set to emerge from\nthe central plains. Ahead of it, return flow will prevail and\nwarmer air will filter in with Saturday high temperatures likely\nto be in the 60s. An attendant cold front is likely to swing\nthrough on Sunday returning rain chances, but so far temperatures\nlook modest behind this front. To aid in a long term pattern, the\nCPC has highlighted the entire Mid-South with a 80-90% chance of\nabove normal temperatures for the end of November into early\nDecember with slightly above (40-50%) chances of above normal\nprecipitation.\n\nDNM",
